138
00:09:36,527 --> 00:09:45,220
Speaker 1: And so what would happen is basically the one time you want to use a VPN, you're most likely to use it when you want to get some internet juice from an untrustworthy network.

139
00:09:46,062 --> 00:09:50,400
Speaker 1: If it's an untrustworthy, malicious, intelligent network, they could be using this attack.

140
00:09:50,500 --> 00:10:01,900
Speaker 1: They say it's been available since 2002 to basically you think you're using your VPN, but you're not and some or all of your traffic is routed, then they can see it and you might not realize it.

141
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:03,282
Speaker 0: So I guess what's?

142
00:10:04,105 --> 00:10:14,120
Speaker 0: a few things stand out to me because one, all modern traffic, anything you're doing, VPN or not, should already either not matter or be TLS encrypted.

143
00:10:14,541 --> 00:10:16,788
Speaker 0: Like, yeah, your content should be safe.

144
00:10:17,130 --> 00:10:23,900
Speaker 0: And two, using a VPN for privacy is kind of stupid because now you're just trusting whoever owns the other end of the VPN to not.

145
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:26,053
Speaker 1: Well, the other end of the VPN could be you as well.

146
00:10:26,114 --> 00:10:26,919
Speaker 1: And then you could trust it.

147
00:10:27,101 --> 00:10:28,640
Speaker 0: Sure, then you can in that rare case.

148
00:10:28,941 --> 00:10:31,512
Speaker 1: That is a separate VPN discussion.

149
00:10:31,552 --> 00:10:33,300
Speaker 1: We're talking about this issue specifically.

150
00:10:34,561 --> 00:10:40,540
Speaker 1: I think we have a pretty easy solution, which is either A, don't allow your routing tables to be changed.

151
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:46,060
Speaker 1: But what if there is actually a real use case where you need it on like a company network or something?

152
00:10:46,681 --> 00:10:48,347
Speaker 1: You won't need it on a public Internet.

153
00:10:48,508 --> 00:10:51,900
Speaker 1: It's like a normal coffee shop isn't going to need that, right?

154
00:10:52,400 --> 00:11:06,360
Speaker 1: Or two, I think there's an easy mitigation, which is even if you allow it, every VPN software out there could just do a simple check of sending some packets and then verifying it if they were VPNed or not.

155
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:08,769
Speaker 0: Yeah, a little more context in through how it looks like this works.

156
00:11:08,849 --> 00:11:11,580
Speaker 0: So it can route some or all of the traffic.

157
00:11:11,660 --> 00:11:12,785
Speaker 0: And I think that's the key point.

158
00:11:12,825 --> 00:11:13,688
Speaker 0: It can be selective.

159
00:11:14,070 --> 00:11:16,319
Speaker 0: So like it could route all the websites you look at.

160
00:11:16,941 --> 00:11:21,259
Speaker 1: Or they could just be like, hey, if anyone does any Facebook logins, route them here.

